            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping along the exterior doors, and around both attic access hatches. 

I recommend to outfit electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. 

I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. There was also air leakage observed from the door sweep of the back door on the first floor. 

Air leakage was also observed through both of the bathroom exhaust fans. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR?????? rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit.

The attic access hatch had quite a bit of air leakage entering from the attic space. I recommend using a removable caulking to seal up the access hatch. 

There was some observable air leakage within the wall shared between the kitchen and upstairs bathroom. I suspect where the rangehood vent passes through the ceiling, the vapour barrier is likely not sealed correctly. I recommend keeping an eye on this area, in the event frost or moisture is observed, this should be addressed. 

Header spaces along the basement, as well as crawlspace. These areas all appear to have fiberglass batt for insulation, however lack the air sealing effectiveness. I recommend removing the fiberglass batts, and adding sprayfoam. 

Upon investigation from outside, the air barrier for the addition on the back of the home appears to have fallen (or potentially ripped). This is likely the cause of this room feeling so cold, as the cold air is able to directly infiltrate into the floor and remove the heat. This air barrier should be replaced and properly sealed.  Another option is to potentially apply sprayfoam (replacing the fiberglass batts currently installed). Sprayfoam, when at least 2 inches (or thicker), will act as both an air barrier, and vapour barrier. 



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $110 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.7 GJ. 



To be eligible for an air sealing rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls of the home.



Savings:

By adding R15 of extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially save $600 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 18.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $5,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



As the front of your home also has brick on the exterior, I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of extruded polystyrene insulation to remaining exterior walls of the home (that does not have brick installed). 



Savings:

By adding R15 of extruded polystyrene insulation to these walls, you could potentially save $510 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 15.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $4,150 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program.</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

Your basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with no insulation. I have modeled adding an interior 2x4 wall, with 3 inches (R15.5) of sprayfoam. 



Savings:

By adding a 2x4 stud wall, with 3 inches (R15.5) of sprayfoam to the basement walls, you could potentially save $930 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 28.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $390 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</foundation>
                <windows>

The majority of your windows are double pane (with the exception of the wood framed ones in the addition), which do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, they are however in reasonable shape, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading the dining room windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3). In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



Savings: 

By replacing all your windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $740 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 22.9 GJ.  Windows that are ENERGY STAR?????? qualified and rated for climate zone 3 are eligible for $ 250 per window in rebates, to a max of 12 windows through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil-fired boiler was originally rated at 85.3% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. 



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $2,200 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 39.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



As you currently have a combination boiler/domestic hot water system, the above savings also include the savings of the increased efficiency of the boiler heating your domestic hot water. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for the boiler will be double that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for a boiler rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



COMBUSTION AIR

I did observe a 4 inch combustion air supply pipe to provide combustion air to the boiler.



I recommend you install an electric damper on the combustion air duct and tie it to the operation of your heating system and water heater. This will ensure fresh air will only be introduced into the room when the heating system (or domestic water heating) is operating. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



WOODSTOVE

By using your woodstove and using it for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially save $900 per year in energy savings (when buying locally @ $420 per cord). 



If you harvest your own wood (estimated at $100 per cord), your savings are estimated to be $2,200 per year. </HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation>

I recommend you replace both toilets. When replacing your toilets, purchase 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilets. 



Savings:

By replacing your toilets with a new 4.8 Liters or less, or dual-flush toilets, you could potentially save ~$50 per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>ECM PUMPS

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house, you can qualify for $175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, and up to 5 ECM pumps per year.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



VENTILATION

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - a 300 CFM range hood   

        - two bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $1.21 per litre 

Propane - $0.68 per litre

Wood - $420 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



